# Course-Beat Slowcore

**Ingredients:** CORSICAN CETERA + SLOWCORE / MINIMAL ROCK + BOWED VIBRAPHONE + FLOOR TOM / BASS DRUM + CONTRALTO CLARINET + REED ORGAN

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during an autumn residency inside a disused chestnut-sorting cooperative in the Balagne region of Corsica, where cetera player Lisandra Orsini was comparing paired-string tunings against an old mechanical grading table while Scottish slowcore drummer Ewan Mercer rehearsed extremely sparse pulse patterns in the adjacent packing room. The useful connection appeared when Lisandra deliberately detuned one string within a doubled course by only a few cents. A single pluck no longer behaved like one clean note: the paired strings produced a slow acoustic beating pattern whose speed changed according to the amount of detuning. Instead of correcting the tuning, the ensemble began composing around the audible rise and fall of those beats. Bowed vibraphone, contralto clarinet, reed organ, floor tom, and bass drum were added to create a slow rock form in which the internal motion of one sustained plucked note can carry more rhythmic information than an entire drum pattern.

Course-Beat Slowcore is a sparse acoustic-electric form built around controlled beating between near-unison strings. Slowcore supplies the structural patience: low tempos, long decay windows, restrained drums, repeated two- or three-chord forms, quiet vocals or instrumental passages, and arrangements that derive intensity from duration rather than density. The Corsican cetera supplies the harmonic engine through its multiple paired metal-string courses. A modern instrument commonly uses sixteen strings arranged as eight double courses; the genre exploits the fact that two strings intended to behave as one pitch can instead be tuned slightly apart so their combined waveform pulses audibly. 

The cetera is recorded with one close small-diaphragm condenser aimed between the soundhole and bridge, one ribbon microphone near the lower bout, and one narrow stereo pair positioned farther back. The close microphone captures plectrum edge, individual course separation, and fret detail. The ribbon preserves body and lower metallic resonance. The distant pair is used primarily to hear the room-scale bloom of the beating pattern after the initial attack has passed.

Players favor open courses, single sustained tones, two-note dyads, sparse octave shapes, minor thirds, suspended seconds, fourths, fifths, natural harmonics, and short repeated figures. Fast strumming is rare. The defining gesture is a hard but controlled pluck followed by complete physical stillness while the two strings within the course move in and out of phase.

The central compositional device is the **Course Drift Table**. Before a piece begins, each doubled course is assigned a target offset: some remain in clean unison, while others are separated by roughly a few cents in either direction. The exact cents value is less important than the audible beating speed. A nearly matched course produces a slow swell-and-fade cycle; a wider offset produces faster throbbing. Compositions move between courses with different beating rates so the internal pulse changes without any drummer increasing tempo.

Unlike reactive electronic systems, the Course Drift Table does not trigger anything. The entire ensemble simply treats the acoustic beating rate as a secondary temporal layer. One section may contain a 54 BPM drum pulse beneath a cetera course whose beating rises and falls approximately three times per bar. Another section may preserve the drum tempo while retuning a course so the beating stretches across multiple measures.

Tuning changes are made manually and audibly when possible. A player may stop between sections, turn one peg slightly, test the course, and continue. Those moments are not theatricalized as sound effects; they function like changing a capo, reed, or drum tuning during a performance. The slight instability is part of the composition.

The rhythm section is intentionally small. A low floor tom, one large bass drum, dry snare, and occasional closed cymbal or brushed metal plate are sufficient. The drummer often plays only one or two events per bar. Rolls are rare. The purpose of percussion is to establish scale and gravity, not to create forward momentum continuously.

Bass drum is tuned low but kept short with felt or blanket damping. Floor tom is allowed a longer decay, especially when its fundamental reinforces one of the cetera's lower courses. Snare is usually played with brushes, rods, or a soft stick near the edge. Cymbal wash is almost absent.

Bowed vibraphone provides a pure sustained reference against the cetera's unstable unisons. Motor vibrato is disabled. The player bows single bars or dyads, favoring fifths, minor sevenths, octaves, and suspended seconds. Because the vibraphone pitch remains fixed, it makes the cetera's beating more obvious whenever one course sits slightly above or below the reference.

Contralto clarinet supplies the low-mid acoustic body. It plays long breath-led fundamentals, slow descending thirds, narrow semitone approaches, split-tone edges, and nearly static pedal notes. It rarely plays melodic runs. Its most important function is to hold one pitch steadily while a cetera course pulses around it.

Reed organ provides long low-volume harmonic beds with minimal registration. One or two stops are used at a time. Open fifths, octaves, suspended seconds, and very plain triads dominate. The organ does not imitate ambient pads; key noise, air movement, and small tuning imperfections remain audible. Its continuous pressure contrasts with the cetera's plucked attack-and-decay envelope.

Electric bass is optional and usually excluded. When present, it uses clean flatwound tone, sparse root notes, and very short phrases. Low-end weight should come primarily from bass drum, contralto clarinet, low cetera courses, and reed organ rather than a conventional rock bass line.

Vocals are low, intimate, and minimally processed. One singer may use close-miked chest voice, nearly spoken phrasing, and long rests. Lyrics favor physical measurements, seasonal labor, failed alignments, family inventories, distances, orchard boundaries, and objects gradually moving out of calibration. Multi-part harmony is rare; when it appears, two voices may intentionally hold slightly different versions of the same note to extend the course-beating principle into the human voice.

Amplification remains modest. The cetera may use a discreet contact pickup blended beneath the microphones for stage reinforcement, but heavy distortion is avoided. If overdrive appears, it is reserved for brief sections where several detuned courses are struck together and their beating products become dense enough to roughen naturally.

The defining tension is stillness versus internal motion. A musician may appear to do almost nothing after striking one note, yet the sound continues changing because two nearly identical strings are repeatedly agreeing and disagreeing in phase.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 44–68 BPM, usually 4/4, 3/4, or very slow 6/8. Drum events are sparse, frequently one bass-drum or floor-tom hit per bar. The cetera's acoustic beat frequency acts as an independent internal pulse that may be faster or slower than the written meter.

**Harmonic Palette:** Aeolian, Dorian, open fifths, suspended 2nds, minor 7ths, bare octaves, modal thirds, pedal tones, close unisons, and near-unisons. Chord progression is slow; most movement comes from course selection, beating rate, register, and decay.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Course Drift Table** — paired cetera strings are assigned different near-unison offsets before or during a composition, producing distinct acoustic beating speeds.
2. **Beat-Rate Form** — sectional development can occur by changing the beating rate while leaving tempo, chord, and drum pattern untouched.
3. **Fixed-Pitch Witness** — bowed vibraphone, reed organ, contralto clarinet, or voice holds a stable pitch beside a slightly detuned course so the interference pattern remains audible.
4. **Silence After Attack** — after major cetera plucks, other instruments deliberately wait long enough for the beating envelope to become perceptible.
5. **Manual Retune** — small peg adjustments between sections are retained as part of performance practice rather than corrected invisibly in post-production.
6. **No-Chorus Inflation Rule** — louder sections may add lower register or wider course spreads, but never solve intensity by adding conventional wall-of-guitars layering.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through wider detuning, lower register, stronger plectrum attack, multiple simultaneously beating courses, harder floor-tom resonance, reed-organ register expansion, and closer clarinet intervals. Climaxes remain slow and exposed, with increased acoustic interference rather than increased note count.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Course-Beat Slowcore formed inside a retired chestnut grading and packing cooperative in northern Corsica. The building retained long wooden sorting tables, mechanical scales, wire baskets, chalk lot numbers, ventilated shutters, canvas sacks, narrow steel conveyors, hand-written harvest ledgers, and pale dust gathered beneath the floorboards.

Ceterist **Lisandra Orsini** had been comparing several reconstructed instruments and alternate tunings. While tuning one doubled course, she stopped before the strings reached perfect unison because the slow pulsing between them sounded unusually pronounced in the dry room. She began timing how long it took the combined note to swell, thin, and return.

Drummer **Ewan Mercer**, visiting through a small rural arts exchange, had been rehearsing slowcore material built from extremely sparse bass-drum and floor-tom patterns. When Lisandra played one detuned course over a 52 BPM pulse, the acoustic beating generated a second rhythm without either musician adding notes. They spent the rest of the session testing different offsets instead of writing chord changes.

Bowed-vibraphone player **Maëlle Santini** joined because her pure sustained tones made the beating easier to hear. Contralto clarinetist **Iona Fraser** added low fixed-pitch witness notes. Reed-organ player and vocalist **Paulu Giudicelli** completed the ensemble with quiet sustained harmony and close-miked text.

Their first performances were called **alignment readings**. A narrow card beside the cetera listed each course and a simple symbol showing clean, slow-beat, medium-beat, or wide-beat tuning. The audience was not expected to understand cent values. The visible chart simply made clear that the wavering sound was deliberate.

The scene developed a visual language of chestnut brown, chalk white, oxidized zinc, faded harvest red, dry olive green, cream ledger paper, black wool, and weak afternoon light through dusty shutters. The unofficial rule is that if a beating pattern cannot be heard after the attack, the arrangement is too busy.

