# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Two Strings Disagree for Eleven Seconds

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 52 BPM in 4/4. Lisandra begins with one low cetera course detuned just enough that its combined tone swells and thins slowly after each pluck. Ewan plays one bass-drum hit every two bars and nothing else for the first minute. Maëlle holds a bowed vibraphone fifth above the cetera, making the low course's wavering envelope unmistakable.

Paulu enters with reed-organ open fifths so quietly that the organ is perceived more as air pressure than chord accompaniment. Iona waits until the third repetition, then holds the pitch that one of the two cetera strings would produce if the course were perfectly unified. The resulting three-way relationship creates a rotating acoustic interference field.

At the midpoint, Lisandra retunes one string slightly closer to its partner. The beating slows without any tempo change. Ewan does not alter the drum pattern. The entire section appears calmer even though the note, register, chord, and written pulse remain essentially unchanged.

The final pluck is left completely alone. The course pulses several times, weakens, and disappears into room tone before the recording ends.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close overhead photograph of one paired cetera course above a chestnut sorting table, two nearly parallel hand-drawn pitch lines drifting together over an eleven-second timeline, mechanical scale and chalk harvest number in shallow focus. Sparse low vocal lines limited to “eleven seconds,” “almost the same,” “do not tighten yet,” and “wait for the second swell.”

## 2. Lot Number Thirty-One Fails the Unison Test

**Story & Context:** At 60 BPM in slow 3/4, Lisandra uses three cetera courses: one perfectly matched, one with a slow beat, and one deliberately wider. The composition alternates between them without changing the underlying two-chord reed-organ harmony. Each course therefore creates a different apparent degree of motion above the same harmonic floor.

Ewan uses floor tom instead of bass drum, striking only the first beat of every second bar. Maëlle stops bowing and plays single damped vibraphone notes after the wider-beating course. Iona sustains a low clarinet pedal through several changes so the pitch reference never resets.

Paulu's vocal describes a harvest lot that repeatedly fails a mechanical sorting tolerance by an almost invisible margin. The lyric is not metaphor-heavy; it reads like a calm inventory record gradually becoming personal.

In the last section, Lisandra plays the clean-unison course and wide-beating course together as a dyad. What appears on paper to be the densest harmony is actually the quietest arrangement, because drums and organ disappear entirely.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cream harvest ledger labeled Lot 31 beside a cetera bridge, three course diagrams marked CLEAN, SLOW, and WIDE, old mechanical sorting gauge resting just outside its tolerance line. Vocal fragments and short verses should use plain language about weight, grade, margin, repeated inspection, and one item that never quite matches the reference.

## 3. Orchard Boundary Moves Without Anyone Walking

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 46 BPM in 6/8. Lisandra begins with high cetera harmonics and almost no obvious beating. Paulu holds a very low reed-organ octave. Ewan plays one soft bass-drum stroke every four measures, allowing the harmonic space to feel nearly meterless.

Every sixteen bars, Lisandra moves to a lower course with a faster beating rate. The written tempo never changes, but the internal motion becomes progressively more active. Iona follows the register downward while remaining on fixed long tones. Maëlle bows two vibraphone notes a minor seventh apart, then gradually removes one note so the ensemble's most unstable section is also its harmonically simplest.

For the final passage, Lisandra retunes two courses during an exposed silence. One beating pattern slows while another speeds up. She then alternates them, making the perceived pulse appear to expand and contract around the unchanged 46 BPM drum skeleton.

Paulu's last line describes an orchard boundary stone found several centimeters from the position written in an old family ledger. No one saw it move. The recording ends on a low cetera course whose two strings never reach perfect agreement.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Twilight chestnut orchard viewed through dusty cooperative shutters, old boundary map on a sorting table with one stone marker drawn slightly away from its original line, cetera strings crossing the map like survey lines. Quiet vocal imagery about measurements, inherited land, centimeters, weather, and positions that change without visible motion; avoid fantasy or supernatural explanation.

